WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.089
Film Courage: Writing every day, is this a
myth or a must for an author?

00:00:04.089 --> 00:00:05.180
Jennifer Brody: It’s kind of a must.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:10.360
I mean, I think that we go through different
periods where we are incubating ideas or working

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:11.360
on ideas.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:16.689
The thing I think about when having a regular
writing practice is that it gets you into

00:00:16.689 --> 00:00:19.470
the mode of producing pages all the time.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:24.439
And I think also for me when I work into a
story, I’m in the world of the story and

00:00:24.439 --> 00:00:27.220
I’m always kind of walking around in that
world.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:30.900
And so it’s a much more seamless process
when I go to sit down to work.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:37.520
If I take a few days off and go back, it takes
time to work back into the story for me.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.960
So I lose time just by being out of it and
then coming back in.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.480
So I think writing everyday will help with
that because it keeps you in the story.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.740
And keeps you moving through it.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:53.600
But I also always say, it’s amazing how
the amount of time you spend writing actually

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:54.920
equals more pages.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.630
It’s not like people think there is some
crazy formula to it.

00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:01.800
I’m like “No it really is time in equals
time out.”

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.200
And some days are going to be better writing
days than others.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:09.290
So it’s not to say that every day you sit
down you’re going to have this amazing output.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:11.980
Some writers have word counts that they do
per day.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.360
I have never done that for myself.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:18.721
A lot of times it is just giving yourself
permission to write what we call (well, I

00:01:18.721 --> 00:01:22.310
won’t say) “The Crappy First Draft”
or “The Bad First Draft.”

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:27.270
So you kind of free yourself from the perfectionism
that is really what Writer’s Block is.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:33.640
Writer’s Block is really feeling the perfectionism
and thinking that writing will be not as good

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:35.420
as what you want it to be.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:41.000
And once the fear of not writing overwhelms
that, that’s usually when we start writing.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.479
So giving yourself permission to write something
that isn’t as good as you want it to be

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:45.979
on the first pass.

00:01:45.979 --> 00:01:48.780
And then a lot of times sitting down and saying
“It’s okay, you can write a paragraph.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.030
That’s okay.”

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:54.210
And then usually once I write a paragraph
I’ll be in it and be writing away.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:57.140
But saying like (if you sit down) “I have
to write 10 pages.”

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:00.620
That can get overwhelming in and of itself,
because that’s a lot of pages.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.990
So just being kind of like a little bit flexible
in how much you are really putting out.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:07.500
But yeah, I would say writing every day is
somewhat important.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.759
Film Courage: And where do you write?

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:11.750
Where do you do your best writing?

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:15.510
Jennifer Brody: It started out where I would
write at home most of the time but now I kind

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:19.700
of call it the Great Coffee Shop Rotation,
which is to say that I move around to a lot

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:20.850
of different coffee shops.

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:25.500
I might have two shifts where one is a coffee
shop and then I come home and work more (especially

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:29.750
when I am under a very crazy deadline like
I am right now).

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:32.480
And there are other writers that I know at
a lot of these coffee shops.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.380
It becomes kind of a community and you know
some of them are screenwriters or doing different

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:38.860
types of things.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:43.160
So I have my friends that I see and it’s
a little bit like going to an office.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:47.990
With being home a lot which some people do
do their best writing, it can be a little

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:52.030
bit where you get distracted because it’s
like the hundred things you need to do like

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:55.430
unload the dishwasher, unload the bed, do
this, do that.

00:02:55.430 --> 00:03:00.160
So leaving the house kind of gets you away
from all of that plus coffee (good coffee).

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.150
So that’s kind of where I do it now.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:06.050
And then I do things like my parents live
in Virginia in the country.

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:10.020
So sometimes I’ll go out there for a couple
of weeks and take it kind of like a working

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:11.020
retreat.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:14.800
I also try to go on a lot of workshops and
residencies.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:19.490
So I did one in Tuscany last year and I am
actually going to Provence to work with some

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:20.490
writers.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:21.670
Actually at Julia Child’s old house.

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:23.400
It’s a cooking and writing retreat.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.180
But I found that sometimes the travel and
being around other authors is really conducive

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:29.180
to creativity.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:32.160
Plus you are getting feedback and you’re
getting workshopping.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.970
So that aspect is nice, too.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:37.450
I always try to do stuff like that.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:40.180
Film Courage: And how, do you write on a laptop?

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:42.180
Do you use just a legal pad?

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:49.900
Jennifer Brody: I write on a laptop and I
use Word, even thought I want to learn Scrivener

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.510
and I hear there is a learning curve and I’m
scared.

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:57.110
Scrivener is a software that a lot of authors
swear by and use.

00:03:57.110 --> 00:04:01.280
And then I write on a laptop but I do have
a little notebook I carry where I will scribble

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:02.280
down ideas.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:07.430
Also, it depends on what stage of writing
I am in, but when I am drafting a story, I’ll

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:11.120
have a lot of ideas that pop up in the middle
of the night or in the shower or when I am

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:12.379
walking.

00:04:12.379 --> 00:04:15.680
So I end up emailing myself a lot of notes.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:21.699
Or even sometimes I’ll sketch out whole
scenes on my cellphone especially if I am

00:04:21.699 --> 00:04:25.330
not at home or with my computer, just like
“Oh…here’s what I am thinking.”

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:29.199
And so I will email it to myself and I’ll
have it.

00:04:29.199 --> 00:04:32.350
Because inspiration pops up at all hours of
the day and the night.

